WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.496
In previous videos, you could see us landscaping&nbsp;around.

00:00:04.496 --> 00:00:09.160
We're taking down trees for good reasons,
 mapping the native trees and protecting them.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.468
All&nbsp;of this to recover the ecosystem,
 and make a land more fire resilient in the long run.

00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:15.640
But, we&nbsp;still have a lot of work to do...

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.180
So, in this video, we are going to improve our&nbsp;land a little bit more.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.940
Welcome to a new Project Kamp update!

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.372
Currently, we are at the top rock area of&nbsp;the land.

00:00:29.372 --> 00:00:33.576
It's a very rocky underground with several types of vegetation.

00:00:33.576 --> 00:00:36.300
Over&nbsp;there, we have the more dense, mimosa area.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.983
Which, by now, we kind of know how to tackle&nbsp;that one.

00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:44.658
But here, we have a more open space,
 with many ferns and native trees in between.

00:00:44.658 --> 00:00:47.744
I can&nbsp;explain you this a bit better,
 if we have a look from the top.

00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:50.001
To give you some context: here is Basekamp.

00:00:50.001 --> 00:00:52.492
Here are the big rocks on the top of the land.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:56.935
This is the area where we are gonna&nbsp;clean up,
 because it has the most native trees in the whole land.

00:00:56.935 --> 00:00:59.249
We want to become a native&nbsp;forest!

00:00:59.249 --> 00:01:04.891
The reason why we are here on this part of the land
 is because next door we have a big&nbsp;eucalyptus farm from the neighbors.

00:01:04.891 --> 00:01:07.518
It's very flammable, that we cannot do anything about!

00:01:07.518 --> 00:01:10.563
But, what we can&nbsp;do something about 
is how we treat our own land.

00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:17.220
For instance: this native tree the cork has been&nbsp;
surviving the fire in 2017, and it's still alive!

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.349
This gives us the idea of how strong native trees&nbsp;can be.

00:01:22.771 --> 00:01:24.239
So, we want some more of them!

00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:25.754
Over there, you can see a madroneo.

00:01:26.507 --> 00:01:31.427
This madroneo is now surrounded&nbsp;by mimosas,
but the mimosa is growing two meters a year.

00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:34.497
So, in a bit, we don't even see the madroneo anymore...

00:01:34.497 --> 00:01:36.600
It will get not get enough sun to grow.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.151
So, this area is mixed with a couple of species.

00:01:40.151 --> 00:01:41.772
For example: eucalyptus.

00:01:41.772 --> 00:01:44.555
A flammable one, 

00:01:44.555 --> 00:01:46.657
And then, we have&nbsp;another of that: the mimosa.

00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:49.447
Both are the same: very flammable, and it's growing very fast too.

00:01:49.447 --> 00:01:54.660
So, we want to control them, and see if&nbsp;
the other ones get enough space to grow up strong.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.871
Even after the fire in 2017, the land&nbsp;didn't get cleared.

00:01:58.871 --> 00:02:04.560
So, there is still all the dead wood from burnt trees
 laying on top&nbsp;of the native trees that are growing up now.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.063
A lot of dead wood here!

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.088
Then, we have these ferns.

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:16.339
They grow very fast, they produce shade, 
but is it good or bad?

00:02:16.339 --> 00:02:17.217
We don't know yet.

00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:18.038
To be figured out!

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.594
So, now, we're currently at the bottom part&nbsp;
of the patch that we are going to work on.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.233
It's close to a very dense mimosa patch.

00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:30.898
But, we're including this area,
 because we still have a lot of native trees growing.

00:02:30.898 --> 00:02:33.163
We would love&nbsp;to include them into the native forest.

00:02:33.826 --> 00:02:35.403
Look over here!

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.874
Okay, so, that is the work!

00:02:40.874 --> 00:02:44.880
It's quite a lot and we are at the height summer,
 which means that it's very hot already.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.765
In this space, we almost have no shade.

00:02:47.765 --> 00:02:50.882
So,&nbsp;it's quite important to find a healthy way
 of working on this area.

00:02:51.966 --> 00:02:56.460
Besides that, we also cannot use all of the power tools,
because it's the fire season.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.560
How to approach this?

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.197
We don't&nbsp;know yet, but luckily,
 we are a big team at the moment!

00:03:02.197 --> 00:03:03.600
Let me&nbsp;introduce you to some people!

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.665
- Hello! My name is Alon!

00:03:08.665 --> 00:03:14.344
I'm 28 years old, and&nbsp;I am currently on a trip across Europe,
 in search of a new place to settle and call home.

00:03:14.344 --> 00:03:18.403
As&nbsp;you can probably already tell, I truly live for danger.

00:03:18.403 --> 00:03:22.984
You'll often find me participating in&nbsp;
some extreme sports such as sewing.

00:03:22.984 --> 00:03:24.060
A job well done!

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.413
Gardening.

00:03:27.317 --> 00:03:30.964
And, drawing the occasional masterpiece.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.691
Nature is where I feel my best.

00:03:33.691 --> 00:03:36.482
I'd like&nbsp;to live simply and sustainably in the future.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:42.797
So, I've come to Project Kamp to learn about&nbsp;this way of living,
 and to see the challenges I may face myself.

00:03:42.797 --> 00:03:46.500
As well as to help out, wherever I can and gain some new experiences!

00:03:53.764 --> 00:03:56.047
- Hello! My name is Doris!

00:03:56.047 --> 00:03:59.189
I'm 26 years old, and I'm from the Netherlands!

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:02.100
I&nbsp;am a painter and also do poetry and spoken words.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.732
I grew up in the middle of a city,
 and&nbsp;nature was something I knew as the grass&nbsp;between the pavement.

00:04:08.732 --> 00:04:12.360
I really love to be in nature for longer periods of time.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.824
That is why I came to Project Kamp!

00:04:15.824 --> 00:04:21.026
Living&nbsp;with much less comfort and materialistic things
 is really inspiring to me!

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:23.760
To really see how creativity flows from that.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.844
- Okay! Enough chit-chat!

00:04:27.844 --> 00:04:29.040
Let's get back to work!

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:05.404
- Good morning!

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.021
- First, we need some tools:

00:05:53.021 --> 00:05:53.880
A brush cutter.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.130
A saw.

00:05:57.576 --> 00:05:58.311
Another saw!

00:05:59.817 --> 00:06:00.657
A lopper.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.058
- This morning, we got up&nbsp;very early,
to start really before the heat is there.

00:06:06.058 --> 00:06:11.065
We're gonna clear this mimosa part&nbsp;
to create some space for us.

00:06:11.065 --> 00:06:13.961
- We divided the whole area in three parts.

00:06:13.961 --> 00:06:16.352
This part we called 'Bottom Mimosa'.

00:06:16.352 --> 00:06:17.820
Okay! Now it's time to start!

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.800
We started by chopping most of the small mimosas,
to access the area with more native trees.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:39.540
Once we opened the entrance path, 
we are able to access the&nbsp;section and continue working.

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:45.542
Here we start to see the native trees!

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.192
- So, it's seven in the morning,
and how are you guys&nbsp;feeling?

00:06:54.818 --> 00:06:57.185
Excited and energised!

00:06:57.637 --> 00:06:58.389
- Yay.

00:06:58.389 --> 00:07:05.015
Let's go free the oaks!

00:07:10.505 --> 00:07:13.056
- As you can see, it's really hard to get around&nbsp;here.

00:07:13.056 --> 00:07:16.205
That's also why we are making these paths.

00:07:19.295 --> 00:07:26.315
A lot of dead trees fell on the young ones...

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:28.680
- Going out.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.880
As you may be able to see behind me,
 we&nbsp;actually found a whole line of smaller oaks.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.038
So, what we're going to do is to free up some&nbsp;space around them.

00:07:42.038 --> 00:07:45.082
This is so they can hopefully grow into a strong native forest.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:08.999
- Did you&nbsp;guys find a new species?

00:08:09.481 --> 00:08:10.987
Yes, we did!

00:08:10.987 --> 00:08:12.422
- What is it?

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.424
Ale, what is it?

00:08:14.725 --> 00:08:22.981
- It's a 'medronheiro', and I think it's called a&nbsp;'strawberry tree', 
with some really bright red fruits!

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.275
We also had to figure out ways to deal&nbsp;with the heat.

00:08:32.275 --> 00:08:33.780
We found some tricks!

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.936
- The sun has come out.

00:08:36.936 --> 00:08:38.634
It's getting hot, girls...

00:08:38.634 --> 00:08:39.134
- It is!

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:43.140
What's up, water boy?

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.700
- Just doing my thing!

00:09:00.875 --> 00:09:03.455
The water boy has company.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.278
In order for us to take all the 
necessary&nbsp;precautions to be fire safe...

00:09:12.278 --> 00:09:18.183
We always bring a bucket with us,
 in case the brush cutters&nbsp;might hit a rock, and ignite a spark.

00:09:18.183 --> 00:09:22.413
It is also something that we use to help us 
cool&nbsp;down when it's really hot.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.400
- There's a bunch of oaks around, 
so everyone&nbsp;has kind of gone and picked a oak to clear.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:38.355
So, I am trying to clear this one.

00:09:38.355 --> 00:09:45.780
I think there's another one hidden here behind me,
so hopefully, in half&nbsp;an hour or so, I have plenty more space.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:53.816
- Ingrid!

00:09:53.816 --> 00:09:54.611
Yes?

00:09:54.611 --> 00:09:57.120
- How do you feel&nbsp;about killing other mimosas?

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.048
Conflicted!

00:10:00.048 --> 00:10:04.710
I know that people have an&nbsp;issue with the mimosas,
 but I only arrived a week ago...

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.738
So, I feel like I need a bit more time?

00:10:07.738 --> 00:10:11.053
They're beautiful trees as well, so I don't know!

00:10:11.053 --> 00:10:13.279
But, I'm glad we can make space for some other&nbsp;types!

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:16.560
- How do you feel about killing the mimosas?

00:10:23.291 --> 00:10:27.780
I think it's&nbsp;quite hard to do so many at the same time.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:34.675
But then, I know from last year,
 that it's super good for the native trees!

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:38.940
To really get some sunlight, and to get a chance to grow.

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:45.011
There's&nbsp;so many out here,
 so I can also imagine it to be like a big forest again.

00:10:45.011 --> 00:10:47.099
So, that's kind of&nbsp;cool!

00:10:47.099 --> 00:10:49.517
- Okay, we only cleared a couple of square meters.

00:10:49.517 --> 00:10:53.836
Even though we only clear around the&nbsp;native trees,
 we have a big pile of green matter already.

00:10:53.836 --> 00:10:58.518
This one we would say is already&nbsp;a bit too high
 to leave behind, because of the risk of fire.

00:10:58.518 --> 00:11:04.179
What we decided now is to&nbsp;make smaller piles
 and mainly on the water lines.

00:11:04.179 --> 00:11:05.750
So, this is one of the examples.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:10.632
Here is a water line where the water rainwater gets collected,
 and runs all the way down.

00:11:10.632 --> 00:11:14.235
To&nbsp;stop this a bit, we would put green matter over here in small piles.

00:11:14.235 --> 00:11:16.560
Hopefully, it&nbsp;stops the rain a bit from flowing.

00:11:26.696 --> 00:11:32.042
Okay, so, as you might see, we placed all the&nbsp;
branches in the area where the water would&nbsp;flow down.

00:11:32.705 --> 00:11:39.144
It might not seem a lot, but&nbsp;in the winter all the water will flow,
 and it this will hold more water.

00:11:39.144 --> 00:11:44.523
So, bit by bit, this will grow and&nbsp;give things,
 like this little oak the space to grow.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:56.284
- As you can see, we already took out a lot&nbsp;
of the wood that was hanging around in the&nbsp;trees.

00:11:56.284 --> 00:12:00.308
We put it on the ground,
so it helps with quicker&nbsp;decomposing. 

00:12:00.308 --> 00:12:03.950
However, there is still this huge pile of wood...

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:08.160
So, we're gonna find ways&nbsp;to use them,
 and make the piles a bit smaller.

00:12:16.027 --> 00:12:21.849
We can also use it to place them&nbsp;
around the native trees that we cleared.

00:12:21.849 --> 00:12:25.679
Just so it retains water, 
and so helps also with recovering&nbsp;the soil.

00:12:25.679 --> 00:12:31.674
We don't take out all the dead wood,
 because some of these trees are still attached&nbsp;to the ground.

00:12:31.674 --> 00:12:36.551
They're actually quite some bugs and birds that really like that.

00:12:36.551 --> 00:12:38.711
So, this&nbsp;one is gonna stay!

00:12:38.711 --> 00:12:43.369
- Besides keeping the dead wood in the ground,
 we want to make an upgrade&nbsp;for the bugs.

00:12:43.369 --> 00:12:44.940
So, we are making a bug hotel!

00:12:59.104 --> 00:13:04.727
We are going to use all of these different&nbsp;
materials as VIP-rooms that bugs like to go into.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.760
So, this is our new bug hotel!

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:27.120
- We are very proud to present the grand opening
 of Project Kamp's first bug hotel!

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:31.942
I knew this would happen!

00:13:32.454 --> 00:13:33.332
Yeah!

00:13:37.688 --> 00:13:39.636
- Okay, enough work for&nbsp;today.

00:13:39.636 --> 00:13:43.028
This day is finished.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:50.824
- Okay, so, at this point, we've&nbsp;completed cutting down
 the first section of this forest.

00:13:50.824 --> 00:13:55.955
We've cut down a lot of the mimosas,
 and created space for ourselves to move around.

00:13:55.955 --> 00:13:58.628
At this point, we have to cut our way up&nbsp;to the big rock.

00:13:58.628 --> 00:14:01.944
This is where things become a little bit more 
treacherous as there is less shade...

00:14:01.944 --> 00:14:02.896
So, it's a lot hotter!

00:14:02.896 --> 00:14:08.180
Plus, we'll be working a lot around the tojo,
 which is this very spiky, brambly bush.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:11.491
It can be quite painful to work with,
 but we should do quite fine!

00:14:11.491 --> 00:14:15.301
- So, for this second area, we called it 'Middle Tojo'.

00:14:15.301 --> 00:14:16.276
Let's start!

00:14:16.276 --> 00:14:17.340
- Good morning!

00:14:30.304 --> 00:14:32.644
We look like NPC's!

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:22.753
- In the summer in Portugal, it is really only&nbsp;allowed 
to use power tools in the mornings, as there is less fire risk.

00:15:22.753 --> 00:15:29.161
So, today, it's a perfect cloudy and cool morning for us
 to bring out the power tools.

00:15:29.161 --> 00:15:31.440
To continue to free some of the native plants.

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.520
- Alon, how do you feel about the spiky stuff?

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:16.200
It's&nbsp;like these spikes are made out of spikes...

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:21.915
- How do your legs feel?

00:16:21.915 --> 00:16:25.644
My legs are in pain!

00:16:25.644 --> 00:16:29.876
- They are and they have swollen,
 but&nbsp;it goes down after a nice cold shower!

00:16:29.876 --> 00:16:30.880
Yeah, they do.

00:16:30.949 --> 00:16:32.360
- Great protection...

00:16:33.535 --> 00:16:37.115
They can grow very big sometimes!

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.894
- So, the tojo is really difficult to&nbsp;work with!

00:16:40.894 --> 00:16:46.861
However, we discovered that it's actually a great sign
 in the first step&nbsp;of regenerating a forest.

00:16:46.861 --> 00:16:52.046
So, the prickly tojo creates protection for the native trees.

00:16:52.046 --> 00:16:53.660
It allows them to grow!

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:58.720
Eventually, as the natives grow and create more shade,
 the&nbsp;tojo naturally dies down again.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:04.940
So, we've cut some of the tojo around the native trees,
to clear up a bit more space.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:09.960
What we can do now is we can replace the cut tojo
around the base to strengthen the soil.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.950
- Up there is the big rock,
 which is what we're trying to reach.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.287
And, on our way there, we're essentially creating
 pathways through the&nbsp;brambles.

00:17:40.287 --> 00:17:45.462
Basically, creating clearings around all of the native trees,
 so that they have the space&nbsp;to grow and flourish.

00:17:46.456 --> 00:17:47.875
- Let's go!

00:17:47.875 --> 00:17:49.294
Let's do it!

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.668
- After making the path, we unlocked a new area!

00:18:11.668 --> 00:18:15.369
So, now we will start to work 
on the most rocky point of the land.

00:18:15.369 --> 00:18:17.605
We called it&nbsp;'the Top Rock'.

00:18:17.605 --> 00:18:19.380
On the map, it's right here.

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:24.718
Okay! So, here is another oak.

00:18:24.718 --> 00:18:26.160
It's time to free it!

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:41.820
Okay, so, as you can see, we not have only a oak,
 but also some little pines!

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:42.847
There is another oak around the corner.

00:18:42.847 --> 00:18:45.987
All freed&nbsp;up and ready to grow!

00:18:45.987 --> 00:18:47.760
- I didn't expect a helmet.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:51.120
We'll wear it for YouTube.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:14.153
- We are almost at the top now

00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:16.882
Right there, we&nbsp;have the land of the neighbors.

00:19:16.882 --> 00:19:20.474
Over there, maybe in the distance, you can see the two big&nbsp;rocks.

00:19:20.474 --> 00:19:25.097
Here, there's still a lot of dead wood lying around 
from all the dead trees.

00:19:25.097 --> 00:19:27.293
So, today,&nbsp;we're gonna clear that.

00:19:27.293 --> 00:19:28.560
Do you feel ready?

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:31.680
- Most certainly!

00:19:53.557 --> 00:19:56.190
Again, we collected the dead wood in piles.

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:59.700
- Otis, do you like being in the General Help team?

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:03.780
It's going really well!

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:18.900
- That was so cool!

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:19.731
I know.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:58.551
- I managed to clear around the trees!

00:20:58.551 --> 00:21:03.117
There are a&nbsp;bunch of branches that were on the floor
 that the ferns had completely covered.

00:21:03.117 --> 00:21:06.660
So, hopefully, they&nbsp;will have now more space,
 and can grow upwards.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.340
There's actually a couple of oaks,
and they have completely intertwined!

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:14.820
They seem to almost be growing out of the rock.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:31.244
So, in the middle of a cluster of young oaks, you'll often find
an old dead wood from the fire five&nbsp;years ago.

00:21:31.244 --> 00:21:35.436
All the new trees branch out from&nbsp;underneath and can grow.

00:21:35.436 --> 00:21:39.887
We're still a little bit unsure, whether to keep them standing&nbsp;
or to take them down.

00:21:39.887 --> 00:21:42.848
If we leave them standing, it is still a bit of a hazard...

00:21:42.848 --> 00:21:44.863
They&nbsp;have a chance of falling over!

00:21:44.863 --> 00:21:46.845
So, in some cases, we're removing them.

00:21:46.845 --> 00:21:49.200
I'm going to see how&nbsp;that goes, and other ones we're leaving up.

00:22:09.872 --> 00:22:11.252
- Oh! What did you find?

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:17.003
I found a vertebrae of an animal.

00:22:17.395 --> 00:22:18.590
- Which animal?

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:23.132
I don't&nbsp;know, but it must have been quite big, I'd say!

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:32.700
- We also found a lot of these clay pots here,
which were used to collect resin from the pine trees.

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:59.520
Guys! Four more minutes of brush cutting!

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.080
- Do a little dance! Yeah!

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.550
So, the clearing of the third section is done.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.990
For now, enough change has been made.

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:28.503
We opened up a lot of space for the native trees.

00:23:28.503 --> 00:23:34.008
With&nbsp;all of these paths around, it would be nice to have some signs 
to teach each others about this area.

00:23:34.008 --> 00:23:36.540
- We're gonna make some signs for the native trees.

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:45.469
We're gonna put the names on wooden plates,
 so&nbsp;we can give some information about other native&nbsp;trees we found.

00:23:45.469 --> 00:23:47.702
- What are we going to use for that?

00:23:47.702 --> 00:23:49.487
Leftover wood!

00:23:54.457 --> 00:24:00.120
We collected some stones now, and now we're gonna crumble it&nbsp;
with this hammer to make it into smaller pieces.

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:15.040
We're gonna add some linseed&nbsp;oil into the pigment.

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:20.642
I use linseed oil, because it's a quick drying oil.

00:24:20.642 --> 00:24:26.482
So, when it meets the oxygen,
 it will go from a liquid to a hard permanent coating.

00:24:26.482 --> 00:24:30.660
It should be very durable!

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:34.260
My first study!

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.020
We can fix it!

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:46.093
- We can fix&nbsp;it.

00:25:52.888 --> 00:25:54.240
Okay, so the work is done!

00:25:54.741 --> 00:25:55.965
Let me show you what we did.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.244
We cleared around the native trees for example.

00:25:59.244 --> 00:26:00.379
Around a meter!

00:26:00.379 --> 00:26:03.701
This oak for example was only visible from the top.

00:26:03.701 --> 00:26:05.045
It was fully covered by tojo.

00:26:05.045 --> 00:26:08.736
We cleared all&nbsp;the tojo around,
and now we have space to grow!

00:26:08.736 --> 00:26:10.612
We did the same with this tree.

00:26:10.612 --> 00:26:11.710
This tree also!

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.259
Two more trees here! 

00:26:13.259 --> 00:26:14.603
Another one over here!

00:26:14.603 --> 00:26:16.031
And, another one over here!

00:26:16.031 --> 00:26:19.017
Besides these, many&nbsp;more in this patch.

00:26:19.017 --> 00:26:22.388
So, you might see some changes, 
but they're not very noticeable yet.

00:26:22.388 --> 00:26:28.379
The real&nbsp;big change will only appear in the long run,
 when all these young trees are growing big into&nbsp;a nice forest.

00:26:28.951 --> 00:26:32.302
Until that happens, they first need still a bit of shade.

00:26:32.302 --> 00:26:36.152
This is why we left&nbsp;some mimosas up,
 and we took the small ones down.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:42.417
We also created some pathways in the area,
to access the spaces more easily.

00:26:42.417 --> 00:26:46.563
This for the maintenance in the future,
but also&nbsp;to live a bit more in the area.

00:26:46.563 --> 00:26:49.913
Because, when you spend time in the place, it stays way nicer!

00:26:49.913 --> 00:26:52.380
A nice roundabout!

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:01.735
So, for the future forest, we already&nbsp;placed in some signs.

00:27:01.735 --> 00:27:03.477
These will identify the trees.

00:27:03.477 --> 00:27:07.980
For example, this Cav arlho&nbsp;Sobreiro, but also many others, like...

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:20.034
So, remember the castanedo in the beginning?

00:27:20.034 --> 00:27:21.328
All&nbsp;super clean!

00:27:21.328 --> 00:27:24.867
We placed all the dead wood around,
 so the bugs still have a place to stay.

00:27:24.867 --> 00:27:28.560
But,&nbsp;they also got a city upgrade which is a hotel!

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:32.895
So, with all the green matter and the dead wood
 that came from the area...

00:27:32.895 --> 00:27:35.102
We created some water retention lines

00:27:35.102 --> 00:27:39.360
This is to help the soil become&nbsp;more furtile,
and to not let everything run off.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:43.095
So, here are some nice stairs for the access&nbsp;to the path.

00:27:43.095 --> 00:27:45.240
Some more water retention lines.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:57.842
To give you an idea of the scale of this project,
we worked with around a group of seven to eight&nbsp;people.

00:27:57.842 --> 00:28:04.260
For around five weeks, and we spent like 400 hours
 on the land, so we did only the mornings.

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:13.137
With a lot of extra scratches, a lot&nbsp;of team building,
 and understanding how&nbsp;to use the tools in such a big space...

00:28:13.679 --> 00:28:15.272
We did a great job!

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:18.470
Okay! That was it for this video!

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.943
The next video is going to be about the pig trailer.

00:28:20.943 --> 00:28:23.488
You can support us on Patreon, and you can see it without ads!

00:28:23.488 --> 00:28:25.362
Or, you wait one week, and see it on YouTube.

00:28:25.633 --> 00:28:27.212
